WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.149
Hi, I'm Tucker.

00:00:01.204 --> 00:00:04.483
This is a short walk through my fourteen months with Grupo Coppel,

00:00:04.716 --> 00:00:06.819
one of Mexico's largest retailers,

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:09.884
as a founding member of their Digital Center of Excellence.

00:00:10.708 --> 00:00:14.181
When I joined, the design team was handing work to a development partner,

00:00:14.181 --> 00:00:16.255
a team from my own company.

00:00:16.568 --> 00:00:18.967
They were reviewing it from a single screenshot in Jira,

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:22.918
not the full set of states and components the designers had built.

00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:25.724
In that role I was there for Coppel and for that team,

00:00:25.724 --> 00:00:28.662
so I made it clear the work gets reviewed in full.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.483
Leadership wanted design on par with the best tech companies.

00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:34.598
The more I looked, the more I saw each

00:00:34.598 --> 00:00:36.895
group only seeing its own piece of the customer.

00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:39.038
Product was a good example.

00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:42.944
Their personas were really buyer personas, built around spending and financing.

00:00:42.944 --> 00:00:46.617
My product counterpart and I brought product managers, some of our UX

00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.984
designers and someone from development together to rebuild them around needs,

00:00:49.984 --> 00:00:53.657
the jobs people were trying to get done, and how they felt.

00:00:54.302 --> 00:00:56.653
Then we walked those personas through a journey workshop.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:59.163
By the end, everyone in the room really saw the value,

00:00:59.336 --> 00:01:01.689
and they'd found gaps they'd never looked for.

00:01:02.512 --> 00:01:04.272
There's no metric on it,

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.768
but the product one-pagers that came after were noticeably better.

00:01:07.281 --> 00:01:09.288
On the design side, a skills-gap assessment showed

00:01:09.288 --> 00:01:10.986
how much room there was to grow.

00:01:11.105 --> 00:01:13.622
I rewrote most of the job descriptions so leaders

00:01:13.622 --> 00:01:15.715
were craft experts with time to mentor,

00:01:15.949 --> 00:01:18.825
and I sat on the panel that hired senior managers against them.

00:01:19.563 --> 00:01:21.812
The assessment also showed a structural problem.

00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:25.652
Six pods each owned one phase, and nobody owned the handoffs between them.

00:01:26.209 --> 00:01:28.741
And design reported to delivery, not outcomes,

00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:31.634
so requirements showed up as decisions already made.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:36.382
I talked it through with product and technology first, then recommended organizing by channel,

00:01:36.382 --> 00:01:40.436
the way engineering already was, with a guild owning the whole experience.

00:01:40.771 --> 00:01:44.532
Leadership agreed, and then it was put on hold behind a CEO-led redesign.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.744
What lasted came from the team.

00:01:46.744 --> 00:01:50.005
In a day-and-a-half workshop, they wrote their own five design principles,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.212
and those are still in Coppel's design system.

00:01:52.986 --> 00:01:56.242
I also made the case for a human-in-the-loop process with AI,

00:01:56.242 --> 00:01:58.116
from product to design.

00:01:58.866 --> 00:02:02.224
Honestly, the tools weren't good enough yet for me to prove it out,

00:02:02.224 --> 00:02:05.800
but I was confident they'd get there quickly, and they did.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.415
Looking back, I think it was the same problem at every level.

00:02:09.415 --> 00:02:11.194
Each group only saw its own piece,

00:02:11.194 --> 00:02:13.982
and getting people to see the whole moved things fastest.

00:02:14.638 --> 00:02:18.148
I used AI to write the team's mobile guidelines, and looking at them now,

00:02:18.148 --> 00:02:21.197
they'd make a great set of files for an AI coding process.

00:02:21.653 --> 00:02:22.453
Thanks for watching.
